WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.180
Thanks to Great Courses Plus for supporting PBS.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.610
Imagine there are two brands of the same product
that are almost identical in quality, but

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:15.209
one costs 3,000 times more than the other…
which do you buy?

00:00:15.209 --> 00:00:20.570
Not exactly a brain-buster, I know, but millions
of Americans fail this test every day.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:25.610
What is this exotic product with the power
to warp people’s financial sense beyond

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:27.300
recognition?

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:29.860
Water.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:44.180
According to the EPA, the average price of
tap water in America is approximately $2 per

00:00:44.199 --> 00:00:45.710
1,000 gallons.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:50.550
Which means that a 16 oz. glass of water will
run you about 3 hundredths of a cent--and

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.640
it’s delivered right into your house, on
demand, 24 hours a day.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:00.100
So how did it become reasonable--fashionable,
even--to buy the same product at prices higher

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.859
than milk or gasoline?

00:01:01.859 --> 00:01:06.480
Since the early 20th century, when advances
in engineering and chemistry enabled cities

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.980
to process and distribute safe, clean drinking
water to millions of residents, bottled water

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:15.750
has been seen primarily as an indulgence of
wealthy elites.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:21.050
However, in the last couple decades, bottled
water has had a remarkable resurgence, thanks

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:25.250
largely to rising concerns over the health
risks of sugary drinks.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:29.510
As sales of Coke and Pepsi have declined,
these companies are trying to make up the

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:34.560
difference by encouraging consumers to buy
bottled water as a healthy alternative.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:35.640
And it’s working.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:41.220
In 1975, the average American only drank about
a gallon and a half of bottled water a year.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.990
Today, that figure is around 30 gallons!

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:50.300
In 2016, sales of bottled water surpassed
even soda, but tap water remains bottled water’s

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:54.210
biggest competitor, with a price advantage
that seems hard to beat.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:02:00.420
So bottlers’ marketing strategy is to present
their product as safer, tastier, and more

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:01.420
convenient.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:02.440
But is it?

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:06.870
Municipal systems typically draw their water
from sources like wells, rivers, lakes, and

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.340
reservoirs.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:11.879
Chemicals are added that bond with dirt and
particles which settle at the bottom of the

00:02:11.879 --> 00:02:12.879
tank.

00:02:12.879 --> 00:02:17.239
The water is then filtered through sand, gravel
and charcoal, and one last round of chemicals

00:02:17.239 --> 00:02:23.079
is added--like chlorine to kill any remaining
parasites, and fluoride to prevent tooth decay.

00:02:23.079 --> 00:02:27.739
Most systems are quality tested dozens of
times a day in accordance with the Safe Drinking

00:02:27.739 --> 00:02:32.849
Water Act, and notwithstanding some recent
tragic contaminations, it’s considered to

00:02:32.849 --> 00:02:34.840
be overwhelmingly safe.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:37.379
So, where does bottled water come from?

00:02:37.379 --> 00:02:40.430
Well, about a third of it comes from the same
place!

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:45.599
Many companies just fill their bottles from
municipal taps after a quick extra filtration.

00:02:45.599 --> 00:02:49.680
After all, 90% of the treatment has already
been done by the city, and they’re selling

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.859
it for less than a penny a gallon!

00:02:51.859 --> 00:02:56.650
The rest of bottled water comes from natural
sources like springs and groundwater, which

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:59.819
can be even cheaper, depending on local regulations.

00:02:59.819 --> 00:03:09.020
In California, Nestlé pays the Forest Service
about $525 annually to extract over 30 million

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:15.069
gallons--which they will eventually sell for
192,000 times more!

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:19.530
While there are health and safety regulations
placed on the bottled water industry, they

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:23.159
are actually less stringent than municipal
ones.

00:03:23.159 --> 00:03:27.159
Bottled water companies are typically allowed
to do most of their own testing, and unlike

00:03:27.159 --> 00:03:31.900
municipal water systems, when they do find
contaminates, they’re often not forced to

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:34.340
do recalls or even alert the public.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:39.219
If bottled water isn’t necessarily safer
than tap, what about the taste?

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:41.510
Well, this may depend on where you live.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:46.560
Tap water in some cities, like Los Angeles,
tends to get poor scores on taste surveys,

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.829
even though tests show it to be as clean--or
cleaner--than bottled water.

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:55.519
New York’s tap water, on the other hand,
routinely beats the pants off bottled water

00:03:55.519 --> 00:03:57.359
in blind taste tests.

00:03:57.359 --> 00:04:02.080
The difference has less to do with quality
and more where it comes from and how it’s

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.080
treated.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:07.300
Convenience outside of the house may be the
only contest that bottled water’s got locked up.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:12.780
12 ounces of ice cold H2O prepackaged in a
lightweight container you can toss in a trash

00:04:12.799 --> 00:04:16.850
can is definitely easier than hunting around
for a public water fountain.

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:21.190
But that convenience comes at a pretty steep
social cost.

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:26.260
About 3.8 million tons of plastic are used
to make water bottles every year, and only

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:29.250
a fraction of that gets recycled.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:34.720
Manufacturing them uses the equivalent of
17 million barrels of oil and 3 times as much

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:36.860
water as actually goes into them.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:42.340
All in all, it’s estimated that bottled
water takes about 2000 times more energy to

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.590
produce and distribute than tap water.

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:48.390
Now, there are some (rare) situations where
bottled water makes sense.

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:53.730
For instance, anytime a natural disaster compromises
municipal supplies, residents have no choice

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:55.440
but to rely on bottled water.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.350
Besides, we drink way too much La Croix to
make any judgements.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:00.350
Is it Croy?

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:01.990
I thought it was Cwah.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:02.990
Cwah.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:03.990
Croy.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:04.990
La Croix.

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:05.990
Croix.

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:06.990
Not Croy.

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:08.440
Not Rue de la Croy.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:09.440
Croix.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:13.410
And if you’ve ever been told by your city
to boil your water before drinking, you know

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:17.630
that city water systems do sometimes fail
safety standards.

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:22.410
This can be caused by pollutants seeping into
the groundwater, or storms washing waste into

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:27.120
reservoirs, but the primary culprit is crumbling
infrastructure.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:31.250
Most of the municipal water systems in the
U.S. were built about a hundred years ago,

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:36.020
and designed to last about a hundred years,
so today America has literally millions of

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:39.390
miles of water pipes that need fixing or replacing.

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:44.070
And with federal funding for infrastructure
at historically low levels, cities are scrambling

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:45.540
to find the money to do it.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:49.220
That’s essentially what happened in Flint,
Michigan in 2014.

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:53.930
City officials tried to save money by switching
to a new water source, and then tried to save

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:58.940
more money by not using corrosion inhibitors
in the aging lead pipes.

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:04.220
Neurotoxic heavy metals seeped into the drinking
water, affecting hundreds of thousands of

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:05.270
residents.

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:10.430
Other cities are trying to avoid Flint’s
fate by raising rates to generate maintenance

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:15.580
funds, leading to an overall increase of 30%
in the average American water bill over the

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:16.580
last decade.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:21.100
That’s a faster increase over inflation
than gasoline or groceries!

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:26.120
But even if you live in a place like Austin,
where water bills have skyrocketed over 150%

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:31.390
in recent years, it’s still much, much,
much cheaper than bottled water.

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:35.270
If you’re still worried about the safety
or flavor of your town’s water, you can

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:40.120
request a Water Quality Report from your water
company to find out where it comes from and

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:41.120
how its treated.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:46.020
Or buy a simple water filter for your home
and a reusable bottle to carry with you, effectively

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:51.210
manufacturing the same product yourself that
sells in stores for thousands of times the

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:52.210
price.

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:56.620
The problem of how to deliver clean drinking
water to millions of consumers was effectively

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:59.170
solved a hundred years ago.

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:03.550
When you buy a bottle of water, you’re only
solving Coke’s problem of how to make up

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:05.260
for lagging soda sales.

